Month = {APR},
Abstract = {The study of reputation is one of the foundational topics of modern
   international relations. However, fundamental questions remain,
   including the question of to whom reputations adhere: states, leaders,
   or both? We offer a theory of influence-specific reputations (ISR) that
   unifies competing accounts of reputation formation. We theorize that
   reputations will adhere more to actors who are more influential in the
   relevant decision-making process. We employ two survey experiments, one
   abstract and one richly detailed involving a U.S.-Iran conflict, to
   evaluate ISR. We find evidence of large country-specific reputations and
   moderately sized leader-specific reputations. Consistent with the theory
   of influence-specific reputations, leader-specific reputations are more
   important when leaders are more influential.},
