Volume = {40},
Number = {1},
Pages = {79-101},
Month = {MAR},
Abstract = {Large partisan gaps in reports of factual beliefs have fueled concerns
   about citizens' competence and ability to hold representatives
   accountable. In three separate studies, we reconsider the evidence for
   one prominent explanation of these gaps-motivated learning. We extend a
   recent study on motivated learning that asks respondents to deduce the
   conclusion supported by numerical data. We offer a random set of
   respondents a small financial incentive to accurately report what they
   have learned. We find that a portion of what is taken as motivated
   learning is instead motivated responding. That is, without incentives,
   some respondents give incorrect but congenial answers when they have
   correct but uncongenial information. Relatedly, respondents exhibit
   little bias in recalling the data. However, incentivizing people to
   faithfully report uncongenial facts increases bias in their judgments of
   the credibility of what they have learned. In all, our findings suggest
   that motivated learning is less common than what the literature
   suggests, but also that there is a whack-a-mole nature to bias, with
   reduction in bias in one place being offset by increase in another
   place.},
